  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C7.S2 — Precision required for continued fractions</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C7.S2 — Sizing the ancilla register</p></div><p>Order finding must resolve \(s/r\) with \(r<N\), where \(N\) has \(L=\lceil\log_2 N\rceil\) bits.</p><p>(a) Legendre\'s theorem requires \(|x-s/r|\le\varepsilon\). Give the largest \(\varepsilon\) that still determines \(s/r\) uniquely, in terms of <code>r</code>.</p><p>\(\varepsilon=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give the number of ancilla qubits \(t\) that guarantees this for every \(r<N=2^{L}\), in terms of <code>L</code>.</p><p>\(t=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) Evaluate \(t\) for an \(L={@L@}\)-bit modulus.</p><p>\(t=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(\varepsilon=\frac{1}{2r^{2}}\): two distinct rationals with denominators below \(r\) differ by at least \(1/r^{2}\), so half that gap identifies the right one.</p><p>(b) Since \(r<2^{L}\), it suffices that \(2^{-t}\le 2^{-(2L+1)}\), i.e. \(t=2L+1\).</p><p>(c) For \(L={@L@}\): \(t=2\cdot{@L@}+1={@ta3@}\).</p><p>For RSA-2048 this is \(t=4097\) ancillas on top of the arithmetic register — before any error-correction overhead. Fault-tolerant estimates instead use windowed arithmetic and semi-classical Fourier readout to bring the logical qubit count down to roughly \(3L\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
