  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C7.S3 — Success probability and repetition</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C7.S3 — How often must we retry?</p></div><p>Assume each independent attempt of Shor\'s algorithm succeeds with probability at least \(1/2\).</p><p>(a) Give the probability that at least one of \(k\) independent attempts succeeds, as a function of <code>k</code> (using the worst case \(1/2\)).</p><p>\(p_k=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give the smallest \(k\) with \(p_k\ge 1-2^{-{@m@}}\).</p><p>\(k=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p><p>(c) Phase estimation returns \(s=0\) with probability \(1/r\), which yields no information. For \(r={@r0@}\), give that probability.</p><p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Failures are independent, so \(p_k=1-2^{-k}\).</p><p>(b) \(1-2^{-k}\ge1-2^{-{@m@}}\) iff \(k\ge{@m@}\), so \(k={@m@}\).</p><p>(c) \(1/r=1/{@r0@}\).</p><p>The overall structure is typical of BQP algorithms: a constant success probability per run amplified to \(1-2^{-m}\) with \(O(m)\) repetitions, at negligible cost compared with the exponential classical alternative. Verification is classical and cheap — multiply the factors back — so the algorithm is a Las Vegas procedure.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
